WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.045
Before developing a database
or a software application,

2
00:00:03.045 --> 00:00:05.775
you first need to plan how
you will organize your data.

3
00:00:05.775 --> 00:00:08.355
This plan is referred
to as a schema.

4
00:00:08.355 --> 00:00:09.930
It's essentially a blueprint

5
00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:11.505
of what your data looks like.

6
00:00:11.505 --> 00:00:13.410
In this video,
you'll learn how to

7
00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:15.855
explain the concept
of a database schema,

8
00:00:15.855 --> 00:00:19.020
identify the different meanings
of the term schema across

9
00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.120
different database systems and

10
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.565
outline the advantages
of a database schema.

11
00:00:23.565 --> 00:00:25.200
Let's begin focusing on what

12
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.795
developers mean when they
use the term schema.

13
00:00:27.795 --> 00:00:30.420
The general meaning of
a schema is that it's

14
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.160
an organization or grouping of

15
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.675
information and the
relationships among them.

16
00:00:34.675 --> 00:00:37.370
In the context of
a MySQL database,

17
00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.265
a schema means a collection
of data structures

18
00:00:40.265 --> 00:00:41.945
or an abstract design of how

19
00:00:41.945 --> 00:00:44.000
data is stored in a database.

20
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.190
Essentially, schema and database

21
00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.895
are interchangeable
terms within MySQL.

22
00:00:48.895 --> 00:00:51.350
A schema is how data
is organized in

23
00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:54.155
the database and how it's
related to other data.

24
00:00:54.155 --> 00:00:55.790
But schema is defined in

25
00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:58.895
different ways across
different database systems.

26
00:00:58.895 --> 00:01:00.635
In a SQL server,

27
00:01:00.635 --> 00:01:02.630
a database schema
is a collection

28
00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:04.775
of different components
like tables,

29
00:01:04.775 --> 00:01:08.080
fields, datatypes, and keys.

30
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.850
In Postgres SQL,

31
00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:12.440
a database schema is a
namespace with the name

32
00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:16.415
database objects like views,
indexes, and functions.

33
00:01:16.415 --> 00:01:18.455
An oracle schema system

34
00:01:18.455 --> 00:01:21.190
assigns a single
schema to each user.

35
00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:25.315
Oracle even names each schema
after its respective user.

36
00:01:25.315 --> 00:01:27.950
But no matter which type
of database you encounter,

37
00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:30.110
the two most important
concepts you need to

38
00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:32.945
understand when working with
schemas remain the same,

39
00:01:32.945 --> 00:01:34.910
organizational data
in the form of

40
00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:38.345
tables and the relationships
between the tables.

41
00:01:38.345 --> 00:01:41.225
Let's now cover the
components of a database.

42
00:01:41.225 --> 00:01:43.820
A SQL Server schema

43
00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.820
is comprised of what are
known as schema objects.

44
00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.160
Many of these objects
will probably already

45
00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.665
be familiar to you from
your study of databases.

46
00:01:51.665 --> 00:01:54.320
They include tables, columns,

47
00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.605
relationships,
datatypes, and keys.

48
00:01:57.605 --> 00:02:00.080
An example of a SQL
database schema

49
00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.779
is a music database
with data and artists,

50
00:02:02.779 --> 00:02:06.170
albums, and genres, all
stored in separate tables.

51
00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:08.180
However, these tables
can still be related

52
00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:10.415
to one another
through various keys.

53
00:02:10.415 --> 00:02:13.235
In other words, the data
within this database

54
00:02:13.235 --> 00:02:16.160
is organized in separate
tables or entities.

55
00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.025
You have the tables are also
related to one another.

56
00:02:19.025 --> 00:02:21.920
Essentially, a database
schema is comprised

57
00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:24.800
of all of the important
data and the relationships,

58
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.980
the unique keys for all
entries in databases,

59
00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:31.390
and a name and datatype for
each column in a table.

60
00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:34.310
Now that you're familiar
with what a database is,

61
00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:35.930
let's move on and explore

62
00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:38.275
the advantages of
a database schema.

63
00:02:38.275 --> 00:02:42.230
Schemas provide logical
groupings for database objects.

64
00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:44.930
They also make it easier
to access and manipulate

65
00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:48.125
these database objects than
other available methods.

66
00:02:48.125 --> 00:02:51.770
Schemas also provide
greater database security.

67
00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:54.530
You can grant permission
to separate and protect

68
00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:57.590
database objects based
on user access rights.

69
00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:00.650
Finally, it's possible
to transfer ownership of

70
00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:01.910
schemas and their objects

71
00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:03.685
between users and other schemas.

72
00:03:03.685 --> 00:03:06.860
In this video, you've learned
that a database schema is

73
00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:08.030
a structure that represents

74
00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:10.115
the storage of data
in a database.

75
00:03:10.115 --> 00:03:12.170
You also now understand
that the meaning of

76
00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:15.010
schema changes across
different database systems.

77
00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:16.700
Lastly, you explored

78
00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.230
the advantages of
a database schema.